{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["In engineering, safety factors often rely on statistical distributions of material strength (inductive), whereas Popperian corroboration rejects such statistics for single-case severity.","Evolutionary fitness is retrospective and blind, while scientific preference is prospective and critically guided by human intent."],"overturn_conditions":["If it can be shown that a less-corroborated theory consistently leads to fewer catastrophic errors in practice.","If the definition of 'severe test' is proven to be inherently circular or dependent on inductive background assumptions."],"shared_structure":["Selection of an option based on past survival under stress rather than predicted future performance.","Acceptance that the selected option may still fail, but failure yields high-information feedback.","Rejection of probabilistic guarantees in favor of robustness checks."],"source_memory_refs":["bridge_engineering_safety_factors","evolutionary_fitness_proxies"],"transfer_claims":["Just as engineers prefer materials with a history of surviving stress tests despite no guarantee they won't fail tomorrow, scientists prefer corroborated theories.","The value of the preference lies in the speed of detecting failure, not the assurance of success."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Popper's preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on his own terms not as an inductive inference of future success, but as a critical methodological rule: acting on the theory that has withstood the severest tests maximizes our exposure to potential falsification, thereby accelerating the growth of knowledge even if it offers no guarantee of practical safety or truth.","counterconditions":["If the domain of action requires absolute certainty of survival rather than maximal learning potential (e.g., building a bridge vs. testing a hypothesis).","If the 'severe test' history is an artifact of lucky background conditions that are known to have changed.","If the cost of being wrong is infinite or catastrophic, rendering the 'learning from error' strategy ethically impermissible."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The mechanism is 'Severe Test Maximization': by choosing the theory that makes the boldest, most precise predictions (and thus has survived the hardest attempts to refute it), we place our bets where error is most easily detected. If the theory fails in action, we learn more than if we had chosen a vague, untested, or already-falsified competitor. This aligns with the aim of science (truth-seeking) without assuming the uniformity of nature.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.65}],"simulation_proposals":[]}